# Safe Deletion Guardrails

Follow the user's account-level git safety rules (no `git reset --hard`,
`git clean -fdx`, or `git push --force` unless explicitly requested).

## Never delete unless asked

Do not use `rm`, the Delete tool, or `git clean` on paths the user did not
explicitly request in the current task.

## Banned without explicit user words

Require clear intent (`delete`, `remove`, `wipe`, `clean up all`) before:

- `rm -rf` on repo root, `config/`, `utils/`, `scripts/`, `docs/`, `.cursor/`
- `git clean -fdx`, `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`
- `docker compose down -v`, `docker volume rm`, `docker system prune -a`
- Deleting or truncating `/data/tyewhong/qagredo/*.tar` or `*.tar.gz` archives
- `bash setup_offline.sh --force` (re-extracts models)

## Protected paths

Do not delete or overwrite without listing paths and getting confirmation:

- `.env`, `certbundle/*.crt`
- `models/`, `models_llm/`, `output/`, `hf_cache`, `hf_cache_judge`
- `/data/tyewhong/qagredo/` (offline archives)
- `QAGREDO_DATA_DIR` mounted data (read-only unless task is data prep)

## Multi-file deletes

Before deleting more than 3 files, or any protected path: list paths and
counts; wait for user confirmation.

## Prefer safe alternatives

- Use `git restore` or rename to `.trash/` instead of permanent delete.
- Use `git mv` for renames; use `git rm` only when removing from version
  control was requested.
- Do not bulk-delete "unused" files during refactors without listing them.
